---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const links = [
  { label: 'Try the live demo', href: 'https://cellar-support.vercel.app' },
  { label: 'Code, evals and write-up on GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/rama-kata/support-agent' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Building cellar"
  description="How I built an AI customer support agent that checks its own work, and what I found along the way."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl py-20 sm:py-28" aria-labelledby="post-title">
    <header class="text-center">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-muted-foreground)] dark:text-[var(--color-muted-foreground-on-dark)]">
        October 2026 · Project write-up
      </p>
      <h1 id="post-title" class="mt-6 text-5xl font-semibold leading-[0.98] tracking-tight sm:text-6xl">
        Building cellar
        <span class="font-display block italic text-[0.9em] text-[var(--color-muted-foreground)] dark:text-[var(--color-muted-foreground-on-dark)]">
          a support agent that checks its own work
        </span>
      </h1>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm font-medium">
        {links.map((l) => <a href={l.href} rel="noopener noreferrer">{l.label} ›</a>)}
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="post mt-16">
      <p>
        I built an AI customer support agent for a wine and spirits store called cellar. It can look up
        orders, cancel them, give refunds up to a limit, check whether something is in stock, and hand a
        conversation to a real person when it should. It runs entirely on my own computer, on two graphics
        cards and local models, and there is a live demo where you can sign in as a customer and try to
        break it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I wanted a project that covered what AI engineer roles actually ask for: building agents, choosing
        between prompting, retrieval and fine-tuning, designing for tool use, building systems that improve
        with use, and shipping it into a real product. So instead of five small demos, I put all of it into
        one thing and measured every part of it.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/writing/cellar/landing.png" alt="The cellar landing page with a live demo of the agent cancelling an order" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>The landing page. The demo on the right is a real order from the demo store.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        The agent is built with LangGraph, with LlamaIndex for searching the store's policies and products,
        and Qwen models served by Ollama. I used a customer support dataset from Kaggle with 27,000 real
        support messages, and real product and sales data from Montgomery County's open data portal, so the
        agent talks about actual products with actual sales history. The Kaggle copy of the sales data only
        covered 10 months, which was too short for the demand forecast, so I pulled the full 24 months from
        the county directly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every message goes through the same steps. It masks card numbers before any model sees them and
        blocks prompt injection, works out what the customer is asking for, looks up the store's policies,
        takes actions with tools that are locked to the signed-in customer, and then checks its own reply
        before the customer sees it. The rule I kept coming back to was: code where an action must happen,
        and the model where judgment helps.
      </p>

      <h2>What I found</h2>

      <h3>My first perfect score was a lie</h3>
      <p>
        I compared three ways of teaching the agent to understand customers, and one of them scored 100% on
        my test set. Instead of celebrating, I dug into it and found that the test questions were almost
        copies of the training questions. The dataset has several versions of each sentence (lowercase,
        no punctuation, typos), and my random split had put versions of the same sentence on both sides.
        After rebuilding the test so each sentence family stays on one side, and removing 3,615 training
        rows that were nearly identical to a test question, the honest score was 97.4%. That taught me to
        be the most suspicious of the results I like the most.
      </p>

      <h3>The agent said it cancelled orders it never touched</h3>
      <p>
        While testing, I caught the agent telling a customer their order was cancelled when it had not
        called the cancel tool at all. That is the worst thing a support agent can do, because the customer
        believes it and still gets charged. Adding more instructions to the prompt did not fix it, so I
        built a check that compares everything the reply claims with what actually happened in the
        database. If they don't match, the agent gets one more try, and if it still can't do it, the
        customer is handed to a person and told honestly that nothing changed yet. One detail surprised me:
        the model ignored my retry note when it was a system message, but followed it reliably when it was
        sent as a user message with only the claimed tool available.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/writing/cellar/chat-confirm.png" alt="The agent asking for confirmation before cancelling an order" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Before anything it can't undo, the agent stops and asks. The details on the card come from the database, not the model.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>Fine-tuning beat prompting the same model</h3>
      <p>
        I fine-tuned a small 3B model with QLoRA on my RTX 3060, which took about 12 minutes, and compared
        it with prompting the exact same model. Prompting got 80.7% of customer intents right. The
        fine-tuned version got 98.1%, and it was faster too, around 50ms instead of 127ms, because it no
        longer needs a long prompt listing every intent. Getting it into production was its own lesson:
        importing the model into Ollama directly produced a model that only answered "????", even though
        the same weights worked perfectly in PyTorch. Converting it with llama.cpp first fixed it.
      </p>

      <h3>Where a model runs changes its answers</h3>
      <p>
        A number in my results didn't match the day before, so I ran it again three times. The fine-tuned
        model scored 98.1% when it ran on the GPU and 99.6% when Ollama pushed it onto the CPU because the
        GPU was full. Both were exactly repeatable. The CPU and GPU round numbers slightly differently, and
        that is enough to flip a few borderline answers. I report the GPU numbers, since that is how the
        demo actually runs.
      </p>

      <h3>My own questions found a gap</h3>
      <p>
        I wrote 40 product questions myself, the way a real customer types, with slang, typos, budgets and
        occasions. The fine-tuned model got 97.5% of them, retrieval got 95%, and prompting got 85%. The
        interesting part was the mistakes. Almost all of them were questions about buying in bulk, like
        "need 2 cases of champagne for a company party, can you do that?", which are about placing an order
        as much as asking about a product. The test didn't just find model errors, it found a place where
        my labels themselves were unclear.
      </p>

      <h3>The agent copied an example from its own instructions</h3>
      <p>
        When a customer asked about "my last order" without a number, the agent looked up order ORD-12345,
        which doesn't exist, and then told the customer the number they gave was wrong. It had copied the
        example order number from its own tool description. I removed the example and made it look up the
        customer's recent orders first.
      </p>

      <h3>The live demo cancelled the wrong order</h3>
      <p>
        After deploying, I tested the live site as a customer with several open orders and asked it to
        cancel my latest one. It cancelled the second newest. The tool returned orders newest first but
        never said so, and the model misread the list. Now the tool names the most recent order directly,
        and the confirmation card shows when the order was placed, so a wrong pick is visible before
        anything happens. My test scenarios had missed this case, so I added it to them.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/writing/cellar/chat-stock.png" alt="The agent answering a stock question with its steps listed in the margin" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Every answer shows how it was reached in the margin, here a catalog search and the demand forecast.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What I learnt</h2>
      <p>
        The biggest lesson was to measure everything before I believe it. Every number on the project
        comes from a test that checks the store's database, not from reading a reply and deciding it looks
        right, and that is how I found most of the problems above. The second lesson was that the important
        rules belong in code. Refund limits, the 21+ age rule, asking before acting and checking claims all
        live in code that the model cannot talk its way around. The third was that real use finds what
        tests miss, so a project is not done when the tests pass, it is done when real people have tried
        it and what they found is fixed.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also built this with Claude Code as my pair programmer, and that taught me something too. It made
        me fast, but the useful skill was directing it, making the technical calls, and checking its work
        instead of just accepting it. Several of the findings above started with me looking at a result
        and asking whether it was actually true.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it stands</h2>
      <p>
        The agent passes 79 of 81 test conversations, including multi-turn, adversarial and wine-knowledge
        cases, and makes the right tool call 98.8% of the time. The two failures are written up in the
        README: a message with two requests in it, and a reply that finds the right order but doesn't name
        it. Next I want to handle messages with more than one request and sort out the bulk-buying labels.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can <a href="https://cellar-support.vercel.app" rel="noopener noreferrer">try the demo</a> while my
        machine is on, and the code, evals and full write-up are
        <a href="https://github.com/rama-kata/support-agent" rel="noopener noreferrer">on GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .post {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-foreground);
  }
  :global(html.dark) .post { color: var(--color-background); }
  .post p { margin: 0 0 1.4em; }
  .post h2 {
    font-family: 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, serif;
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 2.1rem;
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin: 2.4em 0 0.7em;
  }
  .post h3 {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 2em 0 0.5em;
  }
  .post figure { margin: 2.5em 0; }
  .post img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  :global(html.dark) .post img { border-color: var(--color-border-on-dark); }
  .post figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.75em;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--color-muted-foreground);
  }
  :global(html.dark) .post figcaption { color: var(--color-muted-foreground-on-dark); }
</style>
